{"campaign_id": "CAMP-POSTLOCK", "cycle": 1, "index_hash": "6252c5ddde94efc0", "notes": ["roofline-table.md", "megakernels-persistent.md"], "state_seq": 35, "step": "research", "summary": "Baseline profiled on shape 1 at 115 host launch API calls per forward across 13 distinct kernels, against 2 for the graph-replay route, both measured by nsys on the un-graphed and graphed routes respectively. roofline-table.md records shape 1 achieving 11.63 TF, 36 percent of the fp16 peak, and reads every d-128 shape as compute-bound at ideal fusion whose low achieved throughput is a latency and grid problem rather than a bandwidth one. megakernels-persistent.md sets the ceiling honestly: against a CUDA-graphed, block-fused baseline the further upside from persistent kernels is tens of percent at best, concentrated where inter-kernel gaps are a large fraction of runtime. So the first shape-1 direction is the already-built graph replay itself, whose mechanism is now backed by a measured 57x cut in host launch calls rather than by assumption, and the fusion work that the torch-profiler kernel table motivates comes after it, measured against a promoted champion instead of against nothing.", "ts": "2026-08-30T22:28:33+0800"}
{"bottleneck": "launch-overhead", "campaign_id": "CAMP-POSTLOCK", "citations": [{"file": "Project/research/roofline-table.md", "lines": "5-8", "quoted": "| shape | GFLOP | ideal MB | AI | cand ms | achieved TF | vs 32.5 TF | note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 7.52 | 9.2 | 819 | 0.6461 | 11.63 | 36% | healthy |\n| 2 | 0.12 | 0.9 | 128 | 0.1444 | 0.81 | 3% | latency-bound (grid too small for peak) |"}, {"file": "Project/research/megakernels-persistent.md", "lines": "18-24", "quoted": "  inter-kernel gaps are a large fraction of runtime.\n\n## Triton expressiveness boundary\n- Triton has no grid-level sync between programs \u2014 multi-CTA persistent\n  kernels with cross-block dependencies need CUDA C++ (cooperative groups\n  or counter protocols). Single-program kernels need no grid sync and ARE\n  Triton-expressible."}], "counter_evidence_id": "profile-575ff8b9d235cb8378ba8739", "direction": "F-shape1-graph", "falsifier": "If the measured paired speedup lands at or below 1.03, the calibrated promotion threshold for shape 1, then host launch overhead was not a material share of this shape runtime and the graph-replay mechanism is not the shape-1 lever.", "falsifier_kill": "at or below 1.03 paired", "family": {"bottleneck": "launch-overhead", "changed_resource": "kernel-launches", "family_id": "F-shape1-graph", "mechanism": "cuda-graph-replay", "parent_family_id": null}, "hypothesis": "Replaying the entire shape-1 forward as one captured CUDA graph removes the per-call host launch cost that the baseline pays 115 times per forward, and on a shape whose achieved throughput is limited by latency rather than bandwidth that removal should show up as wall-clock win.", "kill": "three non-improving attempts closes F-shape1-graph", "mode": "optimization", "plan_id": "525a0c6372b3", "predict_max": 1.16, "predict_min": 1.14, "prediction": "1.15x expected", "prediction_kind": "win", "prior_family_verdict": "NONE", "reasoning": "The counter is measured on both routes with the same tool, not assumed: nsys on the un-graphed baseline reports 115 launch API calls per forward across 13 distinct kernels, and nsys plus torch-profiler independently agree the graphed route issues 2. roofline-table.md reads shape 1 as compute-bound at ideal fusion with achieved throughput at 36 percent of the fp16 roof, which is a latency and grid problem, so removing 113 host launches per forward attacks the actual limiter. On the band, stated plainly: the gate requires a relative width under 2 percent, so this is a committed point estimate and not the honest width of my uncertainty. The only idle-box evidence this project holds on comparable B-64 d-128 shapes reads 1.14x on shape 9 and 1.56x on shape 10, which bracket far wider than the permitted band, and both are pre-gate numbers that the post-LOCK re-measurement exists to replace. I take the lower anchor, 1.15, because shape 1 carries 4 heads against shape 9 with 1 and its roofline candidate time 0.6461 ms sits closest to shape 9 at 0.6113 ms. A result far above this band is therefore a band miss I expect to have to explain rather than a refutation of the mechanism.", "request_id": "11900d18e6b354829df3d37e07be948f", "request_path": "Project/loop/requests/c7f59c0b93a29e9eb254024e65af645cc3acbca5c97f0340a872966fef8be939.json", "request_sha256": "c7f59c0b93a29e9eb254024e65af645cc3acbca5c97f0340a872966fef8be939", "shape": "1", "state_seq": 36, "step": "plan", "target_sha256": "2feee730569c95b34d6af589a9cfd3d9d861c2419db434b89e5944f0a942ec96", "ts": "2026-08-30T22:30:34+0800"}
