{"campaign_id": "CAMP-POSTLOCK", "cycle": 2, "index_hash": "6252c5ddde94efc0", "notes": ["roofline-table.md", "gemm-epilogue-fusion.md"], "state_seq": 46, "step": "research", "summary": "Attribution correction cycle. The independent audit on attempt 1 returned WEAK_DIAGNOSIS and its arithmetic holds: the bound baseline profile records gpu_busy_ns 114578000 inside gpu_span_ns 118566764 over 20 iterations, so the shape-1 forward is GPU-busy 96.6 percent of its span and only about 0.199 ms of a 5.93 ms forward is host-launch idle. Removing 113 of 115 launches therefore cannot buy more than about 1.035x, essentially the shape 1.03 threshold, while the run reported 2.0748x. Device-busy time itself fell from 5.73 to 2.61 ms per iteration between the two routes, which graph replay cannot cause because it changes only how work is submitted. The remaining explanation is the second change k004 bundles: an authored flash-style Triton attention plus fused packed QKV that removes repeated passes over a 16.8 MB per-layer score tensor, which gemm-epilogue-fusion.md records as the mechanism behind the earlier k010 gain. This cycle therefore opens a fusion family against global-memory-traffic and screens k003, the same Triton attention with no graph capture, to make the split measurable.", "ts": "2026-08-30T23:15:55+0800"}
{"bottleneck": "global-memory-traffic", "campaign_id": "CAMP-POSTLOCK", "citations": [{"file": "Project/research/roofline-table.md", "lines": "5-8", "quoted": "| shape | GFLOP | ideal MB | AI | cand ms | achieved TF | vs 32.5 TF | note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 7.52 | 9.2 | 819 | 0.6461 | 11.63 | 36% | healthy |\n| 2 | 0.12 | 0.9 | 128 | 0.1444 | 0.81 | 3% | latency-bound (grid too small for peak) |"}, {"file": "Project/research/gemm-epilogue-fusion.md", "lines": "1-8", "quoted": "# GEMM epilogue / elementwise fusion (researched 28-29 Aug 2026)\n\n- cuBLAS 12.x fuses common epilogues (bias, GELU) with matmuls:\n  https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/new-cublas-12-0-features-and-matrix-multiplication-performance-on-nvidia-hopper-gpus/\n- Average ~1.45x for GEMMs with epilogue fusion where the elementwise tail\n  was bandwidth-bound (survey via\n  https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/fused-triton-kernels and tile-overlap\n  paper https://arxiv.org/html/2607.02521v1)."}], "counter_evidence_id": "profile-66aaa1f1db26652c96e8c4d4", "direction": "F-shape1-fusion", "falsifier": "If k003 alone lands at or below 1.03, the calibrated shape-1 threshold, then the Triton attention is not the mechanism either and both the graph-replay and the fusion readings of attempt 1 are wrong.", "falsifier_kill": "at or below 1.03 paired", "family": {"bottleneck": "global-memory-traffic", "changed_resource": "launches-and-intermediate-bytes", "family_id": "F-shape1-fusion", "mechanism": "kernel-fusion", "parent_family_id": null}, "hypothesis": "The authored Triton flash attention, with no CUDA graph capture at all, is the mechanism that produced attempt 1 gain. It removes the repeated passes over the materialized 16.8 MB per-layer score tensor that the dense baseline route builds, so it should recover most of the measured speedup on its own.", "kill": "three non-improving attempts closes F-shape1-fusion", "mode": "screening", "plan_id": "2fb9614644d4", "predict_max": 2.12, "predict_min": 2.08, "prediction": "2.10x expected", "prediction_kind": "characterization", "prior_family_verdict": "NONE", "reasoning": "This band is derived from device-busy time on both routes rather than from any speedup row. nsys on the baseline reports 5.729 ms of GPU-busy per forward inside a 5.928 ms span, and torch-profiler on the graphed candidate reports 2.61 ms of GPU-busy per forward. Device work therefore roughly halves, and graph replay cannot cause that because it changes only how work is submitted. k003 runs the identical Triton kernel without capture, so it should do the same device work while still paying the baseline launch idle of about 0.199 ms per forward, giving 5.928 divided by 2.809, or about 2.11. Worth stating because it cuts against my own earlier conclusion: 2.10 predicted from device time is very close to the 2.0748 event speedup attempt 1 actually measured, while the wall speedup of 2.6043 is the arm that disagrees with everything. That suggests the event-timed number was closer to sound than I credited when I read the tripwire, and this screening run on a verified quiet box is the test that separates those readings.", "request_id": "75bddac84bcafc92c99345623de15862", "request_path": "Project/loop/requests/0d963a3e0d0308c17006fceeeab2cd8c1ab10d67e9c31006575bb72055f17411.json", "request_sha256": "0d963a3e0d0308c17006fceeeab2cd8c1ab10d67e9c31006575bb72055f17411", "shape": "1", "state_seq": 47, "step": "plan", "target_sha256": "2feee730569c95b34d6af589a9cfd3d9d861c2419db434b89e5944f0a942ec96", "ts": "2026-08-30T23:17:04+0800"}
